  <model metaid="meta_simvastatin_liver_simvastatin" id="simvastatin_liver_simvastatin" name="Model for simvastatin metabolism in the liver" substanceUnits="mmole" timeUnits="min" volumeUnits="litre" areaUnits="m2" lengthUnits="metre" extentUnits="mmole">
    <notes>
      <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <h1>Model for simvastatin metabolism in the liver</h1>
        <ul>
          <li>Simvastatin (lactone) is taken up passively (reversible) into the
hepatocytes, hydrolyzed to simvastatin acid (esterases, paraoxonases)
and metabolized by CYP3A4/CYP3A5.</li>
          <li>Simvastatin acid is taken up from the plasma by OATP1B1 (SLCO1B1) and
leaves the hepatocytes by efflux; in the liver it is metabolized by
CYP3A4/CYP3A5 and by glucuronidation and CYP2C8. The acid formed in the liver reaches the plasma by the
efflux only, so the activity of OATP1B1 sets the clearance of the plasma
acid (SLCO1B1 c.521CC: AUC of the acid 3.2-fold, Pasanen2006).</li>
          <li>The metabolites (lumped, active and inactive) are exchanged with the
plasma and excreted into the bile (enterohepatic circulation).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The content of this model has been carefully created in a manual research effort.</p>
